5. ACTIVATE ALL THREE SECURITY MODES

Previously, the project had three security modes. Two were disabled temporarily.

Now enable the second and third modes.

First, locate the original mode definitions, their configuration values, and their existing implementations. Preserve the intended behavior rather than guessing what each mode was supposed to do.

The three modes must be available through the Security Dashboard.

Mode 1: Traditional Anti-Nuke

Preserve the existing anti-nuke protection system.

It should focus on detecting and responding to destructive administrative activity, including applicable events such as:

- Mass channel deletion.
- Mass role deletion.
- Unauthorized role or permission changes.
- Mass bans or kicks.
- Webhook abuse.
- Dangerous bot additions.
- Other destructive events already supported by the project.

Use the existing detection logic and configuration wherever possible.

Repair bugs without unnecessarily replacing working detection mechanisms.

Mode 2: Automatic Observability

Enable the second mode.

This is the existing automatic observability system developed for Sentinel.

Give the observability system a new, distinct user-facing name. Do not remove the existing implementation or rename internal modules, database collections, or identifiers indiscriminately.

Inspect the existing codebase for a suitable name already used by the project. If none exists, choose a concise, professional name that communicates continuous security monitoring.

Use that new name consistently in the dashboard, help text, explanations, and configuration labels, while maintaining backward compatibility with existing configuration.

This mode must make the monitoring system available independently of traditional anti-nuke protection.

It should:

- Observe supported security-relevant Discord events.
- Maintain relevant event history.
- Identify suspicious patterns.
- Apply configured thresholds and escalation policies.
- Generate useful incident records.
- Notify the appropriate administrators.
- Execute only the automated responses configured for this mode.
- Explain why an alert or action was triggered.
- Support review and investigation of previous incidents.

Observability must not automatically mean that every detected anomaly warrants punishment.

Separate event collection, detection, alerting, and enforcement into understandable stages.

Use the existing observability architecture wherever it is sound.

Mode 3: Hybrid Security

Enable the third mode.

Hybrid mode must combine traditional anti-nuke protection and automatic observability so that both operate concurrently.

It must not simply display two enabled labels while running only one system.

Requirements:

- Both systems initialize correctly.
- Both systems receive the events they require.
- Both systems maintain their own detection logic and configuration.
- Their results can contribute to a shared incident record.
- Duplicate alerts and conflicting responses are controlled.
- Emergency responses can take priority when immediate action is justified.
- Observability continues to collect useful evidence.
- The dashboard accurately reflects the status of both systems.

Create a clear distinction between an event being observed, an incident being detected, and an automated response being executed.

Mode switching

Users must be able to switch modes through the dashboard.

Implement safe mode transitions:

1. Validate the requested mode.
2. Persist the new configuration.
3. Initialize or disable the appropriate services.
